All stateless components (brig, galley, gundeck, cargohold, spar, cannon,
background-worker, proxy, federator) now spread their pods across availability
zones as well as nodes, matching what nginz already did. The constraints are
soft (`ScheduleAnyway`), so single-zone clusters and clusters whose nodes carry
no `topology.kubernetes.io/zone` label are unaffected. Each component's
`topologySpreadConstraints` value can be overridden, or set to `[]` to opt out.

Even zone spread is a prerequisite for zone-aware routing: with the
`service.kubernetes.io/topology-mode: Auto` annotation these charts already set,
Kubernetes publishes no topology hints at all -- silently routing across every
zone -- unless endpoints are distributed in proportion to each zone's
allocatable CPU. Relying on the scheduler to land on that distribution by
itself meant one unlucky rollout or drained node could take hints away from a
whole Service, with nothing to alert on it; on cloud providers that bill
cross-AZ traffic in both directions, that shows up on the bill.

Additionally, a new opt-in `global.trafficDistribution` value (empty by
default) sets `spec.trafficDistribution` on the Services of the `wire-server`
chart and its `nginz`, `proxy` and `federator` subcharts -- one switch for the
whole release. Set it to `PreferClose` to have kube-proxy prefer same-zone
endpoints; it supersedes the `topology-mode: Auto` annotation and is more
predictable, because it decides per endpoint instead of refusing to emit hints
at all. Requires Kubernetes >= 1.31 (beta) or >= 1.33 (GA); enabling it on an
older cluster fails at template time rather than silently doing nothing. Only
enable it once every component is spread across zones, since it has no
per-zone capacity heuristic.
